
1. What is Terraform ?
Terraform is an Infrastructure as Code (IaC) tool used to provision, manage and automate cloud infrastructure using code

2. Which language Terraform uses to write codes ?
Terraform uses HCL (HashiCorp Configuration Language) which is human‑readable and declarative

3. What all .tf files we need in working directory and their use ?

-- main.tf
✅ Contains core infrastructure resources (EC2, VPC, etc.)

-- variables.tf
✅ Defines input variables to make code dynamic and reusable

-- outputs.tf
✅ Displays output values (IP, DNS, IDs)

-- provider.tf
✅ Defines cloud provider (AWS, Azure, etc.) and region

-- terraform.tfvars
✅ Stores actual variable values

4. What terraform init actually does ?

✅ Reads .tf files
✅ Downloads required providers (AWS, Azure, etc.)
✅ Initializes backend (state storage like S3)
✅ Creates .terraform/ directory
✅ Prepares working directory for execution

5. How terraform communicates to cloud providers ?
Terraform uses Providers (plugins) to communicate with cloud platforms via their APIs
Example:
AWS provider → interacts with AWS APIs
Azure provider → interacts with Azure APIs

6. Explain terraform project directory structure ?
terraform-project/
│
├── main.tf
├── variables.tf
├── outputs.tf
├── provider.tf
├── terraform.tfvars
│
├── modules/
│   ├── vpc/
│   │   ├── main.tf
│   │   ├── variables.tf
│   │   └── outputs.tf
│
├── .terraform/
├── terraform.tfstate
├── terraform.tfstate.backup


-- Root files
✅ main.tf → Main resources definition
✅ variables.tf → Variable declarations
✅ outputs.tf → Output values
✅ provider.tf → Provider configuration
✅ terraform.tfvars → Variable values

-- Modules
✅ Reusable components (VPC, EC2, DB)
✅ Helps in modular and scalable design

-- .terraform/
✅ Stores downloaded providers/plugins
✅ Created after terraform init

-- terraform.tfstate
✅ Tracks real infrastructure state
✅ Maps Terraform code ↔ actual resources

-- terraform.tfstate.backup
✅ Backup of previous state

-- Best Practice
✅ Use remote backend (S3 + DynamoDB locking)
✅ Do not commit state file in Git
✅ Use modules for reusable code
✅ Keep environments separate (dev, prod)
✅ Follow folder structure for scalability

7. How to limit creation of specific resource when many resources are defined ?
-- Command
✅ terraform apply -target=module.ec2
-- What it does
✅ Applies only the targeted resource/module
✅ Skips other resources in the execution plan
-- Use case
✅ Debugging specific module
✅ Creating only required resource (partial deploy)

8. How to destroy a particular target resource ?
-- Command
✅ terraform destroy -target=module.ec2
-- What it does
✅ Destroys only the specified resource/module

-- Problems of using -target
❗ Breaks dependency graph
❗ May leave orphan/dangling resources
❗ Not recommended for regular use

-- Best Practice
✅ Use full terraform apply/destroy whenever possible
✅ Use -target only for troubleshooting

9. What is the use of count in Terraform ?
-- Definition
count is used to create multiple instances OR conditionally create resource

-- Use cases
✅ Create multiple resources
✅ Enable/Disable resource (ON/OFF switch)

-- Example
count = 3 → creates 3 instances
count = 0 → skips resource

-- Interview one-liner
👉 count acts like a loop + conditional creation mechanism

