✅ Terraform Lifecycle Block (Deep + Interview Ready)
Definition
Lifecycle block controls how Terraform handles creation, update, and deletion of resources.
👉 It is used to override default behavior and avoid production issues.

1. lifecycle block syntax
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = "ami-123"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"

  lifecycle {
    create_before_destroy = true
    prevent_destroy       = true
    ignore_changes        = [tags]
  }
}


2. Key lifecycle arguments

✅ create_before_destroy
-- Default behavior
❌ Destroy old → then create new
-- With lifecycle
✅ Create new → then destroy old
-- Use case
✅ Zero downtime deployment
-- Example
lifecycle {
  create_before_destroy = true
}

👉 Real scenario:
Updating EC2 AMI → ensure new instance starts before old one terminates

✅ prevent_destroy
-- Definition
Prevents accidental deletion
-- Example
lifecycle {
  prevent_destroy = true
}

👉 Real scenario:
Production DB → don’t allow deletion even if someone runs terraform destroy

✅ ignore_changes
-- Definition
Ignore changes in specific attributes
-- Example
lifecycle {
  ignore_changes = [tags]
}

👉 Real scenario:
Tags updated manually by another team → Terraform should not revert

✅ replace_triggered_by (advanced)
-- Definition
Force recreation when another resource changes
lifecycle {
  replace_triggered_by = [aws_ami.latest]
}

👉 Use case:
Auto recreate EC2 when AMI changes

✅ Lifecycle Block – Interview Summary
👉 Controls how Terraform behaves during changes
👉 Helps avoid downtime, data loss, and unwanted updates

